menu "CAN (canbus)"

    config CANBUS_RX_QUEUE_LEN
        int "Frames the receive queue holds"
        default 256
        range 16 1024
        help
            Between the controller's interrupt and whoever reads the frames (the `can` command,
            or the Cyphal node). 24 bytes each, in internal RAM, from the first start on.

            A flash write holds the reader off while the interrupt goes on filling the queue:
            an erase runs for up to CONFIG_SPI_FLASH_ERASE_YIELD_DURATION_MS (20 ms) before it
            yields. Measured 2026-11-20 with a bus saturated at 1 Mbit/s by 8-byte frames
            (about 8,100 a second) during a 1 MB LittleFS write (`fs bench 1024`): the queue
            held 155 and 164 frames at most in two runs, and a 128-frame queue dropped 997 frames
            over the write. 256 covers that with room. A bus saturated with shorter frames (up to
            about 21,000 a second with no data) would need more; a Cyphal bus is normally far
            lighter.

    config CANBUS_TX_QUEUE_LEN
        int "Frames waiting to be sent"
        default 16
        range 2 255
        help
            Frames given to canbus_send() and not yet on the bus. When they are all waiting
            (nobody acknowledging, or bus-off), canbus_send() waits for room or times out.

endmenu
